\section{Introduction}

The aim of this assignment is to apply the framework proposed by Basagaña et al. to the data set \texttt{copd} in the file \texttt{copd\_redux.RData}. Data \texttt{copd} is a subset of 45 variables of the original data analyzed in the cited paper.

\section{Analysis}
\subsection{Description of missingness}
Provide the same missingness description than in the original analysis. Specifically, reproduce the following text that is shown in the original paper:\\[10pt]
The required packages are:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Number of participants;
  \item Number of variables;
  \item Missing data percentage of each variable;
  \item Missing data percentage of less than $5\%$ of missing;
  \item Percentage of variables with all variables without any missing;
  \item Percentage among all cell with missing data.
\end{itemize}
<<Description_of_missingness, include=TRUE, echo = TRUE>>=
load("copd_redux.RData")
n <- length(copd$id) #participant number
v <- ncol(copd) #variables number
M <- n*v #cell number

missing <- colSums(is.na(copd)) 
miss_percentatge <- (missing/n)*100 #missing 

minimum_missing_pp <- min(miss_percentatge) #minimum missing percentage
maximum_missing_pp <- max(miss_percentatge) #maximum missing percentage

miss_under_5 <- sum(miss_percentatge < 5)
pctg_under_5 <- (miss_under_5/v) * 100
#percentage of variables with less than 5% missing

complete <- sum(miss_percentatge == 0)
complete_pctg <- (complete/v)*100
#percentage of patients with complete data on all variables

overall <- sum(is.na(copd))
overall_pctg <- (overall/M)*100 
#Overall percentage of the information is missing
@
Now we complete the missing values of the text:\\

“The subset used here included \textcolor{red}{\Sexpr{n}} participants and \textcolor{red}{\Sexpr{v}} variables. The range of missing data in the different variables went from \textcolor{red}{\Sexpr{v}}\%, with \textcolor{red}{\Sexpr{minimum_missing_pp}}\% to \textcolor{red}{\Sexpr{sprintf("%.1f",maximum_missing_pp)}}\%, with \textcolor{red}{\Sexpr{sprintf("%.1f", pctg_under_5)}}\% of the variables having less than 5\% of the data missing. Only \textcolor{red}{\Sexpr{sprintf("%.1f", complete_pctg)}} of the participants had complete data on all  \textcolor{red}{\Sexpr{v}} variables. Overall, \textcolor{red}{\Sexpr{sprintf("%.1f", complete_pctg)}} of the information was missing; that is, \textcolor{red}{\Sexpr{sprintf("%.1f", overall_pctg)}}\% of  \textcolor{red}{\Sexpr{prettyNum(M, big.mark = ",")}} cells ($\textcolor{red}{\Sexpr{v}} \times \textcolor{red}{\Sexpr{n}} = \textcolor{red}{\Sexpr{prettyNum(M, big.mark = ",")}}$) had missing data.”

\subsection{Multiple imputation}
Apply multiple imputation to \texttt{copd} data using 100 imputations. Explain in a reasoned manner how you decided to set the arguments \texttt{method} and \texttt{predictorMatrix} when using the \texttt{mice()} function.\\[5pt]

Before staring to decide the arguments for \texttt{method} and \texttt{predictorMatrix} we should do a little summary to know the classes of each variable of this \texttt{copd} data-set. 
<<Multiple_imputation_1, include = TRUE, echo = TRUE>>=
str(copd)
@
As we see, most of the variables are numerical, however there are some variables which are factors. Their names and corresponding numbers of levels are listed below:
<<Multiple_imputation_2, include=TRUE, echo=TRUE>>=
categoricas <- names(copd)[sapply(copd, is.factor)]
categoricas_latex <- gsub("_", "\\\\_", categoricas)
niveles <- sapply(copd[categoricas], nlevels)
binarias <- names(niveles[niveles == 2])
binarias_latex <- gsub("_", "\\\\_", binarias)
no_binarias <- names(niveles[niveles > 2])

@
As we can see, this data set has \Sexpr{length(categoricas)} variables that are
factors, and their names are \texttt{\Sexpr{paste(categoricas_latex, collapse = ", ")}}. The following factors are binary distributed: \texttt{\Sexpr{paste(binarias_latex, collapse = ", ")}}



